// iplinear — the two methods that do not smooth anything.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// WHY THESE ARE NOT IN `ipspline`.  They are the two methods a caller
// reaches for when they do NOT want a curve: piecewise linear is what a
// plot's line segments are and what `numpy.interp` does, and nearest is
// what a lookup table does.  Neither has a boundary condition, neither
// can overshoot, and neither needs more than two points.  Putting them
// beside the cubic methods would have made a reader wonder which
// boundary condition applies to them.
//
// THEY ANSWER AN `IpCurve` ALL THE SAME, because everything downstream —
// `at`, `at_many`, `derivative`, `integral`, `resample` — is written
// once over that type.  A piecewise linear curve is a cubic whose
// quadratic and cubic coefficients are zero, and a nearest one is a
// cubic whose top three are.  The representation costs two multiplies
// per evaluation that a specialised form would not do, and buys every
// downstream function for free.
//
// NEITHER CONSTRUCTOR IS FALLIBLE, and that is the point of `IpKnots`:
// the validation happened there, two points is the minimum `IpKnots`
// already enforces, and there is nothing left for these to refuse.

// The piecewise linear curve through the knots.
//
// `numpy.interp`, with the extrapolation policy named rather than
// defaulted.  The straight line between each pair of knots, continuous
// everywhere and differentiable nowhere in particular.
//
// Total: `IpKnots` guarantees at least two points and everything else
// this needs.
//
// ```novo
// use ipknots
// use iplinear
// use ipcurve
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match ipknots.of_lists([0.0, 1.0, 2.0], [0.0, 1.0, 0.0])
//         Ok(k) =>
//             let c = iplinear.of_knots(k, IpExtrapClamp)
//             println("${ipcurve.at(c, 0.5)}")   // 0.5
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn of_knots(k: IpKnots, e: IpExtrap) -> IpCurve []
    todo("interpolate-nv.iplinear.of_knots")

// The piecewise constant curve: the value of the NEARER knot.
//
// A lookup table with a tie rule.  At the exact midpoint of an interval
// it answers the RIGHT knot's value — scipy's `kind="nearest"` rounds
// half up in the same way, and a rule stated is worth more here than a
// rule that is right, because a caller sampling a step function on a
// grid that lands on midpoints will see every one of them.
//
// `kind="nearest-up"` and `kind="previous"` and `kind="next"` are
// scipy's three other step variants and are NOT here; the README names
// them.
pub fn nearest(k: IpKnots, e: IpExtrap) -> IpCurve []
    todo("interpolate-nv.iplinear.nearest")

// The linear interpolation of one point, with no curve built.
//
// The one-shot form, for a caller who has two lists and one `x` and is
// not coming back — `numpy.interp` with a scalar argument.  It does the
// binary search and the arithmetic and keeps nothing.
//
// Fallible because it validates the knots on the way through: this is
// the one path into the package that does not start with `IpKnots`, so
// the check happens here instead.
//
// **One shot.**  A caller with more than one `x` wants `of_knots` once
// and `ipcurve.at_many`, which is a validated construction and then a
// single pass.
//
// ```novo
// use iplinear
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match iplinear.interp([0.0, 2.0], [0.0, 4.0], 1.0, IpExtrapClamp)
//         Ok(y)  => println("${y}")   // 2
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn interp(xs: [Float], ys: [Float], x: Float, e: IpExtrap) -> Result<Float, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.iplinear.interp")

// The linear interpolation of many points, with no curve kept.
//
// The other one-shot form, and the direct translation of
// `numpy.interp(new_xs, xs, ys)`.  It validates once and then makes one
// pass, so it is not the trap `interp` in a loop would be — it is here
// so that a port from Python is a rename rather than a restructure.
pub fn interp_many(xs: [Float], ys: [Float], at: [Float],
                   e: IpExtrap) -> Result<[Float], IpFault> []
